15
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:05,440
It is around 24 times cheaper than Haiku and around 230 times cheaper than Fabo5.1.

16
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:08,800
And a big part of why that is is because it's output tokens,

17
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:12,800
basically it's response to you as the user is always free.

18
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,720
And they only charge for the input tokens, which is essentially your prompt.

19
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:19,920
And it's super cheap, it's only 4 cents per million tokens.

20
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,200
Now a big part of why it's so fast and so inexpensive

21
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,240
is because Jev can only answer in three shapes.

22
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:29,360
So when you ask a question, it can either give you a binary response,

23
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:31,280
whether that statement is true or false.

24
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:34,480
It can provide a selection against a menu of options.

25
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:38,960
Or it can also give you a response that is based on scale, let's say from 0 to 10.

26
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,840
And even though this sounds like a big limitation for the model,

27
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:46,560
this is actually the genius behind why it's so effective in the use cases that we'll go through later.

28
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:49,920
But before we go to that, one key thing to remember is that Jev

29
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,480
is not actually a large language model.

30
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,920
And the way that typesafe, which is the company behind Jev talks about this,

31
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:59,120
is that they're saying that Jev is the first system one model,

32
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:03,200
whereas all the other AI models like Fable, Astra, and the others

33
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,440
are what they're called in system two models.

34
00:02:05,440 --> 00:02:08,160
Now in case you've read this book called Thinking Fast and Slow,

35
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:12,400
that system one and system two dichotomy of how humans and people think

36
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:13,600
might be familiar to you.

37
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:16,800
But essentially the difference between these systems is that system one

38
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,600
is all about thinking fast and making snap decisions.

39
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,800
And so Jev as an AI model just optimizes against this.

40
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:26,640
And so it just outputs classifications and can do that really, really fast

41
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:28,080
and really, really cheaply.

42
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:31,920
Whereas LLMs and other general purpose models like Fable or Astra,

43
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:35,440
they can output text and they do that by writing each word one by one.

44
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:38,880
But it gives them more flexibility of what it can output, obviously.

45
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:41,920
But that would have the drawback of these system two models

46
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,960
thinking slower versus its system one counterparts.

